courses.topical_outline:
The burgeoning field of animal studies in literature continues to innovate. Therefore, individual texts and topics might vary from year to year. In all of the possible versions, the attention to animals as forming our relationship to the environment is inevitable. 

One variation of the course, sampled below, would focus on the “discourse of species” and its relationship to otherness. Reading both from critical theory and literature, the course would offer an interdisciplinary view on how humans have framed animality and the environment in the twentieth century.  

Other variations of this course might focus on the influence of “animal rights” in thinking about animality, reading Upton Sinclair, Jack London, JM Coetzee, Jonathan Safran Foer, Cora Diamond, Thomas Nagel, Emmanuel Levinas, and others. Because scholarship on animal studies ranges widely, other iterations of the course might focus on specific literary periods or more specific themes: animality and material history, children’s literature and fables, animals in visual culture, etc. 

Sample schedule and possible readings:
The Animal “Other” UNIT 1: The Human Zoo: Animals and Race (4 weeks) 

Literature: Franz Kafka, “Report to an Academy” (1917); Suzan-Lori Parks, Venus (1995); HG Wells – Island of Dr. Moreau (1896);

Criticism: Selections from Randy Malamud, An Introduction to Animals in Visual Culture (2012); Steve Baker “Sloughing the Human” (2000); Kalpana Rahita Seshadri, selections from HumAnimal: Race, Law, Language (2012). UNIT 2: Animals and Modernity (3 weeks)

Literature: Eugene O’Neill, Hairy Ape (1923); DH Lawrence, “The Fox” (1922); Virginia Woolf, Flush (1933); 

Criticism: Akira Lippit, “…From Wild Technology to Electric Animal” (2002); Caroline Hovanec, selections from Animal Subjects (2018); UNIT 3: Animals and Sexuality (3 weeks)

Literature: Marie Darrieussecq, Pig Tales (1996); Marianne Moore, “Paper Nautilus” (1940); 

Criticism: Donna Haraway, “Teddy Bear Patriarchy” (1989); Carol J. Adams, from Sexual Politics of Meat (1990); Susan McHugh, “Bitches from Brazil” (2002). UNIT 4: Animals and Ecology (4 weeks)

Literature: Indra Sinha, Animal’s People (2007); Laline Paul, The Bees (2014); 

Criticism: Andrew C Isenberg, “The Moral Ecology of Wildlife” (2002); Jane Desmond, “Requiem for Roadkill” (2016); Holly Duggan and Scott Maisano, “Ape” from Veer Ecology (2017).

courses.course_objectives:
By the end of this course, students will understand the materials and techniques of building construction. By the end of this course, students will  understand the physical forces acting on a building, especially those that tend toward deterioration By the end of this course, students will understand the history of building construction, so as to be able to: 
a) date buildings or portions of buildings by means of construction techniques; 
b) understand current problems experienced by historic structures. By the end of this course, students will know the resources available for the treatment of building material conservation problems. By the end of this course, students will be able to recognize and diagnose the most prevalent conservation problems experienced by historic structures. By the end of this course, students will be able to make at least general recommendations for the solution of the most prevalent conservation problems. By the end of this course, students will be able to participate in the research and writing of a historic structures report. By the end of this course, students will understand the values inherent in contemporary preservation practice towards building conservation, especially as these are embodied in the Secretary of the Interior's Standards for Rehabilitation and Restoration.

